 \section{Introduction}
 \label{sec:intro}
+Counterfactual auditing asks whether a policy's value or a subgroup's outcome would satisfy a declared requirement under a target exposure distribution. A point estimate cannot answer this question when rare logged exposures or correlated records make its uncertainty large. The relevant distinction is between a valid certificate and a certificate narrow enough to support a decision.
 
-Logged decision systems increasingly learn from data generated by earlier versions of themselves. A logging policy selects an exposure, only the corresponding outcome is observed, and the resulting log is reused to evaluate or constrain a target policy. The relevant questions extend beyond average policy value: is a score calibrated under an intervention, does a policy violate a safety constraint, are direct or spillover effects unequal across subgroups, and can a hidden failure population be detected? Each can be expressed as a \emph{counterfactual moment}: an intervention-specific orthogonal score multiplied by an auditor or constraint function.
+We study a finite family of counterfactual moments under a fixed exogenous evaluation design. Exposure propensities are known, evaluation macro-blocks are independent, and a sufficient dependency graph describes dependence within each block. Policies, auditors and outcome predictions are fixed before evaluation; an independent pilot may supply them. This model permits weak positive overlap and finite-$p$ score moments while keeping the conditioning needed for finite-sample inference explicit.
 
-Reliable inference is difficult when three pathologies interact. \emph{Weak overlap} produces large importance ratios for actions rarely chosen by the logging policy, even with bounded rewards. Outcomes such as watch time, transaction value, and medical cost may themselves be heavy-tailed. Finally, units are dependent through shared slates, repeated users, local interference, and temporal feedback. Doubly robust estimation \citep{dudik2011doubly,jiang2016doubly,farajtabar2018more}, weak-overlap analysis \citep{wang2017optimal,mehrabi2024weak,khan2024beyond}, robust mean estimation \citep{lugosi2019mean}, and causal inference under interference \citep{aronow2017estimating,savje2021average} address important parts of this problem, but do not by themselves provide simultaneous, operational certificates for families of counterfactual questions in dependent logs.
+\COMET\ provides two separate inference routes. A graph-adjusted median of means (MOM) uses a known conditional moment envelope and a common target across blocks. Its number of groups can be chosen by an exact binomial majority bound. For bounded outcomes, a graph-aware ordinary mean instead uses a known variance upper bound and Chebyshev's inequality. Each procedure spends its own confidence budget over the whole frozen family. Neither choosing the narrower observed interval nor pooling their guarantees is justified without an additional argument.
 
-We introduce \textbf{\COMET} (\textbf{CO}unterfactual \textbf{M}oment \textbf{E}stimation and \textbf{T}esting). A generalized exposure mapping converts a joint assignment into a local exposure state; a cross-fitted orthogonal score identifies a target intervention; a logged conflict graph records dependence induced by slates, entities, and declared interference neighborhoods; and graph--time median-of-means aggregation produces simultaneous confidence radii under only a finite $p$th moment, $p\in(1,2]$. The target is a predictable window average, so adaptive deployment and within-window drift are visible rather than hidden inside a stationary population mean.
+The contribution is an explicit proof-to-computation contract for these counterfactual certificates: the target, dependence assumptions, constants, confidence budget, feasible group counts, and refusal conditions are stated together. The underlying DR identity, MOM argument, graph coloring and pessimistic selection are established tools, not new concentration principles. An exact-replication subclass supplies an expected-risk lower bound with an explicit constant; it does not establish graph-specific optimality. On a simultaneous coverage event, the same moment interface supports held-out subgroup tests and local constrained selection. Empirical discovery and repair remain separate future studies.
 
